            "text": "There is a particular kind of quiet in a kitchen before anyone else wakes up. The refrigerator hums with the confidence of a machine that has never doubted its purpose. A spoon rests beside the sink. Outside, the sky is still deciding whether to become blue.\n\nI like the idea that a day begins without instructions. Nothing has happened yet, but everything is available: an apology, a walk, a sentence worth keeping. Even the most ordinary morning contains doors we cannot see until we touch them.\n\nSomeone will miss a bus and meet a friend. Someone will finally throw away a broken chair. Someone will find an old photograph and remember that their parents were once young enough to be uncertain about everything.\n\nMeanwhile, bread goes into the toaster.\n\nPerhaps this is what makes ordinary life so difficult to describe: its scale keeps changing. We worry about the future while searching for a clean mug. We carry grief into grocery stores. We fall in love and still need to buy toothpaste. The enormous and the trivial share a countertop.\n\nThere is no lesson hidden here, unless it is that attention can be a form of affection. To notice the steam rising from a cup is not to solve anything. It is simply to stay, for a moment, with the world as it is.\n\nSoon the house will fill with voices. The spoon will disappear into a drawer. Morning will become Tuesday.\n\nFor now, the toast is warm.\n\nThat is something."
          }
        ],
